# compiler issue: the native backend's range pass (back/opt/ranges.lucb) is cubic in the number
# of bounds checks that dominate each other in one function.
#
# luce-base 0.23.0 on an M-series Mac. Each bounds check that is not removed adds a Fact, and
# Search.at_most walks every fact (and, through `equal`, every fact again) for each later check,
# recursing to depth 3. `luce-base test --native` of this file (one function with 1000 indexed
# loads, each with its own index) takes 3.9 s; with 500 loads 0.7 s, with 2000 loads 27.7 s
# (x7 per doubling). The same happens to a module's test runner: the test blocks are called once
# from `main`, the inliner expands them there, and their checks all dominate the later ones. A
# file of 600 test blocks with 4 `assert(values[index] == 3)` each takes 24 s, 1200 blocks 188 s
# (`luce-base test` picks the native backend when the module has `asm`, as ak and gc do).
# luce-browser-foundation's ak module (about 650 tests) then takes many minutes to compile.
#
# Expected: roughly linear time (bound the facts searched per check, or index them by value).
# Actual:   cubic time in the number of dominating checks.
# Workaround: none in the code; luce-browser-foundation's test.sh waits for ak's test build.

func sum(values: const u64[], indexes: const usize[]) -> u64:
    var total: u64 = 0
    total +%= values[indexes[0]]
    total +%= values[indexes[1]]
    total +%= values[indexes[2]]
    total +%= values[indexes[3]]
    total +%= values[indexes[4]]
    total +%= values[indexes[5]]
    total +%= values[indexes[6]]
    total +%= values[indexes[7]]
    total +%= values[indexes[8]]
    total +%= values[indexes[9]]
    total +%= values[indexes[10]]
    total +%= values[indexes[11]]
    total +%= values[indexes[12]]
    total +%= values[indexes[13]]
    total +%= values[indexes[14]]
